{"step_index":1885,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:32:37Z","content":"Here is the exact, step-by-step breakdown of how the local database is architected, initialized, and executed.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Core Architecture: Dual-Engine Design\n\nBecause this application runs both as a **Native Mobile App (iOS / Android)** and as a **Web Progressive App ([`valuteachers-dialer.surge.sh`](https://valuteachers-dialer.surge.sh))**, the database layer uses an **Interface Adapter Pattern** defined in [`src/db/database.ts`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/database.ts).\n\nAll higher-level services ([`LeadsRepository`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/leadsRepository.ts), [`CallLogsRepository`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/db/callLogsRepository.ts), etc.) interact with a unified contract:\n\n```typescript\nexport interface IDatabase {\n  getAllAsync<T>(sql: string, params?: any[]): Promise<T[]>;\n  getFirstAsync<T>(sql: string, params?: any[]): Promise<T | null>;\n  runAsync(sql: string, params?: any[]): Promise<any>;\n  withTransactionAsync(fn: () => Promise<void>): Promise<void>;\n  execAsync(sql: string): Promise<void>;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Initiates the Database?\n\nWhenever any screen opens (e.g., [`ListsScreen`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/screens/ListsScreen.tsx) loading leads, or [`ScanSheetScreen`](file:///C:/Users/brorm/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/snap-dialer/src/screens/ScanSheetScreen.tsx) saving a scanned card), it calls `getDatabase()`.\n\n`getDatabase()` is a lazy singleton factory:\n1. It checks if an active database connection instance (`dbInstance`) already exists in memory.\n2. If not, it detects the runtime environment using `Platform.OS`.\n3. If running on **iOS or Android**, it opens the **Native SQLite Engine**.\n4. If running on **Web / Browser**, it boots the **IndexedDB Engine**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Is It In Fact SQLite?\n\n#### A. On iOS & Android (Native): **YES — 100% Native SQLite**\n- **Library:** `expo-sqlite` (which compiles SQLite C-source\n<truncated 1191 bytes>\ng., `AppData/Local/Google/Chrome/User Data/.../IndexedDB`).\n- **Capacity:** Multi-gigabyte storage (can use up to 80% of your free disk space).\n- **Persistent Locking:** The engine requests `navigator.storage.persist()`, instructing the operating system and browser never to clear or purge this database during background cache sweeps.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Database Schema Breakdown\n\nBoth the SQLite tables and the IndexedDB collections store the exact same relational structures:\n\n| Table / Collection | Purpose | Key Columns / Fields |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`leads`** | Primary educator records & card data | `id`, `name`, `phone`, `school`, `planning_period`, `years_in_frs`, `priority_tier`, `last_disposition`, `source_image_uri` |\n| **`call_logs`** | Complete call attempt history & notes | `id`, `lead_id`, `timestamp`, `disposition`, `note`, `sms_sent`, `duration_seconds` |\n| **`lists`** | Named campaign lists & pipeline batches | `id`, `title`, `total_leads`, `completed_leads`, `created_at` |\n| **`cadence_rules`** | Multi-touch follow-up rules | `id`, `step_number`, `condition_disposition`, `action_type`, `delay_hours` |\n| **`sms_templates`** | ValuTeachers & FRS personalized texts | `id`, `title`, `body` |\n| **`settings`** | API keys, caller signature, auto-pacing | `key`, `value` |\n\n---\n\n### 5. Summary of Data Flow\n\n```\nUser Action (Scan Card / Log Call)\n                 │\n                 ▼\n        LeadsRepository / CallLogsRepository\n                 │\n                 ▼\n          getDatabase() Singleton\n                 │\n       ┌─────────┴─────────┐\n       ▼                   ▼\n [Mobile OS]           [Web Browser]\nexpo-sqlite           IndexedDB Storage\n(WAL Journal .db)     (Persistent Multi-GB Disk)\n```\n\nIn summary:\n- **On Mobile:** It is a true SQLite database with Write-Ahead Logging.\n- **On Web:** It is an IndexedDB persistent disk storage engine.\n- **Both:** Provide complete offline data persistence, surviving page refreshes, app updates, and restarts.","thinking":"**Clarifying Database Implementation**\n\nI'm currently focused on dissecting the local database implementation. I'm aiming to provide a comprehensive explanation of how the database is initiated and structured, specifically addressing whether it's SQLite, as suggested. I'm carefully avoiding any modifications to the codebase, solely seeking to clarify the existing setup.\n\n\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":1886,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:35:07Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nwould it be better just to keep this a pwa?  i only wanted it mobile os specific to account for the sqlite.  if that's not necessary, would it be advisable and more effective and more cross compatible to focus it as a pwa?\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-08-23T09:35:07-04:00.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
{"step_index":1887,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:35:07Z","content":"**Yes, focusing primarily on a Progressive Web App (PWA) is an outstanding, highly strategic move for ValuTeachers.** \n\nIn fact, for field sales, educator outreach, and fast iteration, a **PWA offers massive advantages** over maintaining separate native app store binaries.\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Why PWA is the Superior Choice for This Workflow\n\n#### 1. Instant Field Updates (Zero App Store Delays)\n- **App Store Frustrations:** Native apps require packaging (`.ipa` / `.apk`), provisioning certificates, and waiting 2–5 business days for Apple App Store and Google Play reviews for every single bug fix or text template tweak.\n- **PWA Advantage:** You deploy in 5 seconds (`npx surge`). When reps open the app, they immediately have the latest updates, FRS objection handles, and features with zero manual downloads.\n\n#### 2. 1-Tap \"Add to Home Screen\" (Indistinguishable from Native)\n- On **iPhone (Safari)**: Tap *Share* ➔ *\"Add to Home Screen\"*.\n- On **Android (Chrome)**: Tap *Menu* ➔ *\"Install App\"*.\n- **The Result:** It opens in full-screen standalone mode with its own custom gold & emerald app icon, no browser URL bar, full haptic feedback, and camera access.\n\n#### 3. True Universal Cross-Compatibility\n- **In the Field:** Rep dials leads on an iPhone or Android phone during planning periods.\n- **At the Desk / Home:** Rep opens the exact same URL on a Windows PC, Mac, or iPad to view reports card scans, export Excel/CSV pipelines, or review notes on a larger monitor.\n\n#### 4. Native Telephony & SMS Integration Works Seamlessly\n- **1-Tap Phone Calls:** The dial button launches the phone's native cellular dialer (`tel:`) instantly.\n- **1-Tap AI SMS:** Tapping \"Draft Text\" opens the native Apple iMessage or Android Messages app with the teacher's name and personalized FRS copy pre-filled.\n\n#### 5. Multi-Gigabyte IndexedDB Eliminates the Need for SQLite\n- The primary historical reason for building native apps was local database storage. \n- With **IndexedDB**, modern browsers provide a multi-gigabyte, transactional, persistent disk database on the device that works offline—delivering all the benefits of SQLite without native compilation overhead.\n\n---\n\n### 📊 Direct Comparison: PWA vs. Native App\n\n| Feature | PWA (Web Focused) | Native iOS / Android App |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Deployment Speed** | ⚡ **Instant (5 seconds via Surge/Vercel)** | ⏳ 2–5 days (Apple/Google App Store review) |\n| **Cross-Device Access** | 📱 iPhone, Android, iPad, Mac, Windows PC | 📱 Phone only (must install from store) |\n| **Field Card Scanning** | 📷 High-Res Camera Capture | 📷 High-Res Camera Capture |\n| **1-Tap Dialing (`tel:`)** | ✅ Fully supported | ✅ Fully supported |\n| **1-Tap AI SMS (`sms:`)** | ✅ Fully supported | ✅ Fully supported |\n| **Offline Storage** | 🛡️ **IndexedDB (Multi-GB persistent)** | 🛡️ SQLite (`.db` file) |\n| **Friction for Field Reps** | 🔗 Open URL & bookmark | 🔐 Requires Apple ID, store credentials, updates |\n\n---\n\n### 💡 Recommendation\n\n**Focus 100% on the PWA as your primary production platform.**\n\nBecause our codebase uses **React Native for Web**, you maintain the best of both worlds:\n1. You get maximum speed, universal compatibility across all phones and computers, and instant deployment right now.\n2. If you ever decide in the future that you want a listing in the Apple App Store, the exact same codebase can be compiled into a native binary with one command (`npx eas build`).","thinking":"**Considering PWA Advantages**\n\nI'm evaluating whether to stick with a PWA approach. The main driver for mobile-specific development was SQLite, but I'm reconsidering that constraint. Exploring cross-compatibility and overall effectiveness, I'm leaning toward the advantages a PWA might offer.\n\n\n**Evaluating Architecture Options**\n\nI'm now deep into comparing a pure PWA approach to native mobile and hybrid/universal solutions. The instant deployment and cross-platform flexibility of a PWA look increasingly appealing. Zero app store overhead and the \"Add to Home Screen\" experience are significant advantages for our reps. Instant updates are a huge win. The comparison is becoming very compelling.\n\n\n"}
